#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518} :activity/id #uuid "3b059c68-9dd5-5f61-a9e4-d2768be710f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058 :topic/id #uuid "d6879e2a-6593-5a32-bdad-609e580b017a" :topic/math-academy-id 1048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097841 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/67/673ea82819858b9a46e0e351a8d6f41b069ed039ee0d31a382110f938f7ef72a.svg)\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n![](images/35/35627a034b30736abb057c01f3251fc047dce780a8cbcf389f8b5c2c2317d766.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/32/32a252e05e299a206f2f3776a2a04419f21726e1f8f542cfd4f328dac3251f21.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d8cb1c1-9e09-560f-83ad-7c8a3d0a78cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3155 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54dae6c6-bb01-588b-b012-dc097a5b2c65" :step/math-academy-id 17196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097842 :tutorial/content "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n- If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n- We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n\n- if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n- If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n- We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n- If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n- In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "955c2b9a-3d5f-52c3-b4f3-62541732ffcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3156 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "4f927453-918f-5476-ba92-06cc1a2e3f72" :step/math-academy-id 17197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459 :question/id #uuid "b77c065c-3821-513c-9376-6e7108074616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395769}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "8c5555a5-f06e-5c31-9de6-ec1aef27abbb" :step/math-academy-id 17198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460 :question/id #uuid "7760b6ce-91fb-5ba6-beb2-c244d6a73d69" :question/math-academy-id "e-4702"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414031}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"} :step/id #uuid "813ae7dd-cdf7-5b02-ad5e-3911c5f08ece" :step/math-academy-id 17199}] :activity/title "Solving 3x3 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096856}]}
